<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Pilar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1820</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentine-civil-wars">Argentine Civil Wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Pilar was signed on 23 February 1820 by the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, Manuel de Sarratea, and the federalist caudillos <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-ramirez">Francisco Ramírez</a> of Entre Ríos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estanislao-lopez">Estanislao López</a> of Santa Fe, after their victory at Cepeda. It ended the central Directorate, accepted a federal organisation for the provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> and declared free navigation of the rivers. By ignoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Artigas</a>, it broke up the Federal League.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-pilar.jpg" alt="Treaty of Pilar" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gobernadores de Buenos Aires y de la Liga Federal · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TratadoPilar1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
